00:03:28.302 --> 00:03:32.487
from, right, the fact that we've got here
is because we have those two things.

00:03:32.487 --> 00:03:35.699
And in fact, it's interesting,
I looked this up,

00:03:35.699 --> 00:03:40.371
at no point in history have you all had
a better likelihood of living a long and

00:03:40.371 --> 00:03:45.240
fruitful life, just on average and
in specific, very interesting.

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.523
All right, after all the complaining,

00:03:47.523 --> 00:03:50.893
your expected outcome is higher
in almost every category.

00:03:53.463 --> 00:03:55.082
I don't know, it's interesting,

00:03:55.082 --> 00:03:58.520
a child born today is expected to
live about 90 years, all right?

00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:02.050
Children born by the mid-century
are expected to live at least 100 years,

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:03.995
pretty good news.

00:04:03.995 --> 00:04:06.680
That's average, right,
we all understand averages.

00:04:08.490 --> 00:04:12.940
And by the way, when you have such a long
expectancy, my core advice is that you

00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:15.605
need to brush your teeth because
you're going to need them, right?

00:04:15.605 --> 00:04:18.260
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So let's establish some basic rules

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:21.190
here, you have to think
about these things.

00:04:21.190 --> 00:04:26.284
And the world has this interesting way
of changing, so when I graduated from

00:04:26.284 --> 00:04:31.792
college in the 1970s, I graduated as
an engineer, an electrical engineer.

00:04:31.792 --> 00:04:35.366
And there were lengthy studies of the fact
that there would never be jobs for

00:04:35.366 --> 00:04:37.040
engineers in the future.

00:04:37.040 --> 00:04:40.727
And in fact, I was going into a job
market where it was not obvious that

00:04:40.727 --> 00:04:44.303
engineers would have jobs at all,
we might be doing other things.

00:04:44.303 --> 00:04:47.010
Of course, we wanted to be engineers,
but I pressed forward.

00:04:48.050 --> 00:04:52.250
The computer that I used in
college has as its replacement,

00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:58.383
in roughly the same building, a computer
that is 100 million times more powerful.

00:04:58.383 --> 00:05:04.960
I went to visit it, right, that's a lot
of change, right, in that period of time.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:09.510
So if we're in this sort of age of
abundance that I'm describing now,

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:12.420
what happens after this,
how does this transition?

00:05:13.550 --> 00:05:18.150
And I'm convinced that the age
of abundance leads to the age

00:05:18.150 --> 00:05:21.470
of intelligence, and I'll tell you why.

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:24.409
It has a lot of implications,
probably the defining thing for

00:05:24.409 --> 00:05:27.870
your business careers and, perhaps,
for your personal lives as well.

00:05:29.470 --> 00:05:33.530
The technological boom, the parts that
many of us have involved with, people here

00:05:33.530 --> 00:05:37.040
in the room and myself, obviously,
have produced the following facts.

00:05:39.450 --> 00:05:46.357
Computer vision is now better than
human vision even if you're drunk,

00:05:46.357 --> 00:05:51.895
right, it's still better,
that's a pretty big deal.

00:05:54.649 --> 00:06:00.247
The analytical systems that are being
built now can do better planning and

00:06:00.247 --> 00:06:04.131
analysis for
any system that has variable inputs,

00:06:04.131 --> 00:06:09.610
that is variable supply, and
variable demanders, right?

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:12.638
Much of the business and
dynamic world does that, and you say,

00:06:12.638 --> 00:06:14.870
well, why is he talking about this?

00:06:14.870 --> 00:06:17.020
Because that will underpin
much of what you do.

00:06:18.490 --> 00:06:23.200
In medicine, it turns out, shockingly,
we're all the same, right,

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:25.685
all of us, all human beings, right?

00:06:25.685 --> 00:06:29.899
If you go to enough of us and
you do enough analysis of our healthcare

00:06:29.899 --> 00:06:33.300
outcomes, you can materially
improve healthcare.

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:37.730
Whether it's precision medicine or
better drug discovery,

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:41.492
the technology is such that as
the technology that AlphaGo, for example,

00:06:41.492 --> 00:06:45.135
represents, which is the reinforcement
learning that won the game against Go, for

00:06:45.135 --> 00:06:46.810
those of you who don't know,
it's a very big deal,

00:06:48.070 --> 00:06:52.400
is analogous to the kind of technology
you would use to look at candidates for

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:55.800
doing protein folding to
apply to generate new drugs.

00:06:57.030 --> 00:06:59.430
So you go from winning a game to, perhaps,

00:06:59.430 --> 00:07:04.100
winning the real game, which is making
us healthier and solving diseases.

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:07.630
So what does this mean for you all?

00:07:07.630 --> 00:07:12.040
Well, lets talk about cars,
all right, how many of you

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:16.220
know someone who's had a tragic family
loss, somebody died in a car accident?

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:21.114
I think that in your lifetimes,
and perhaps sooner,

00:07:21.114 --> 00:07:26.666
we'll get to the point where cars are as
safe as flying on commercial airliners,

00:07:26.666 --> 00:07:31.993
I won't make a joke about that, in terms
of accidents in the air, all right?

00:07:31.993 --> 00:07:36.086
Basically, when you get on
a commercial airliner today,

00:07:36.086 --> 00:07:40.107
it's safer than it is driving
to the airport in your car.

00:07:40.107 --> 00:07:45.411
So the engineering, and the capability,
and the ability to do computer vision and

00:07:45.411 --> 00:07:49.197
so forth will, in fact,
take something that is, really,

00:07:49.197 --> 00:07:54.499
a crude way of saying it is that 90 people
are scheduled to die today driving cars,

00:07:54.499 --> 00:07:56.370
we just don't know who yet.

00:07:57.710 --> 00:07:59.760
It's a horrific thing to say, isn't it?

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:03.000
&gt;&gt; But those are the numbers.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:07.780
And by the way, they're going up for
various reasons.

00:08:07.780 --> 00:08:10.616
We need to fix this and
it'll get fixed very soon.

00:08:10.616 --> 00:08:13.070
In the next few years,
the process will really begin.

00:08:14.800 --> 00:08:19.260
So if you think about vision as a solved
problem, then what does that do for

00:08:19.260 --> 00:08:20.940
radiology and pathology?

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:23.269
So when you go to the dermatologist,

00:08:23.269 --> 00:08:28.004
you really want the dermatologist to
use a computer assisted way of looking

00:08:28.004 --> 00:08:32.456
at your skin to make the diagnosis to
help you get healthy and so forth.

00:08:32.456 --> 00:08:33.429
Why?

00:08:33.429 --> 00:08:36.474
Because the computer can see more
skin than the ophthalmologist.

00:08:36.474 --> 00:08:39.459
It can literally see more eyes,
if it's diabetic retinopathy.

00:08:40.820 --> 00:08:46.180
So I am convinced that these
technologies that I'm describing are so

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:52.420
profoundly powerful, that they
will underpin the creation of some

00:08:52.420 --> 00:08:58.270
fantastic large new corporations that
are just in the process of being founded.

00:08:58.270 --> 00:09:00.118
It's always been true in my career.

00:09:00.118 --> 00:09:02.944
Now, this is my fifth or
sixth cycle doing this.

00:09:02.944 --> 00:09:08.638
That when you get a wave like this, the
kind of things that I'm talking about that

00:09:08.638 --> 00:09:14.000
the great new corporations get founded
by entrepreneurs who take advantage

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:19.361
of the things that we've learned here and
the principles of open markets and

00:09:19.361 --> 00:09:24.200
the capital investment and so
forth and we wrote a book about this.

00:09:24.200 --> 00:09:30.745
So when I think about it, what is
the emerging tech model of success?

00:09:30.745 --> 00:09:32.746
It's no longer slow product cycles.

00:09:32.746 --> 00:09:36.350
It's fast iteration learning,
your product, your app changes every week.

00:09:37.610 --> 00:09:40.620
Extremely deep sensing in telemetry data

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:42.550
on everything that you care
about in your business.

00:09:43.610 --> 00:09:44.970
Making the customer or

00:09:44.970 --> 00:09:49.370
using the customer's information to
educate you in learning from them.

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:52.255
Remember, if you've got a million
customers educating you and

00:09:52.255 --> 00:09:54.659
you use machine learning,
you can learn from them and

00:09:54.659 --> 00:09:56.598
become better than any single one of them.

00:09:56.598 --> 00:10:00.810
You make them better and you get
the benefit of their training of you.

00:10:00.810 --> 00:10:04.598
So any solution where
there's a large training set,

00:10:04.598 --> 00:10:09.734
a large community, these technologies
will transform the economics,

00:10:09.734 --> 00:10:13.534
the potential, the brands and
the impact they have.

00:10:13.534 --> 00:10:17.514
And to do this, of course, you put
everything into the Google Cloud or

00:10:17.514 --> 00:10:19.007
perhaps Amazon if not us.

00:10:19.007 --> 00:10:23.140
And you used tents or flow, which is a new
library that we released to do this.

00:10:23.140 --> 00:10:26.710
And I think that it's possible that
these next generation of companies,

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:31.735
the one's that you and other will found
can get to 90% market share models,

00:10:31.735 --> 00:10:33.790
because you move so quickly.

00:10:33.790 --> 00:10:36.710
That success model has not slowed down.

00:10:37.900 --> 00:10:40.760
You read the press and everybody goes
well, there's this big company and

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:41.830
that big company.

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:47.883
There's never been more opportunity
than now to create these companies,

00:10:47.883 --> 00:10:51.256
because the barriers to entry are so low.

00:10:51.256 --> 00:10:54.523
The tools are so powerful and
the need is so great, and

00:10:54.523 --> 00:10:58.450
we can talk about this some more
with questions, and comments.

00:10:59.620 --> 00:11:03.160
So, I thought I would talk a little
bit about being a student here.

00:11:04.900 --> 00:11:06.850
Being a graduate.

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:10.920
Being what you will be and
I thought about it for a while,

00:11:10.920 --> 00:11:12.780
and I thought this is
a great year to be you.

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:18.800
You managed to get here and
you're either about to graduate or

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:20.050
about to graduate in a year.

00:11:21.800 --> 00:11:26.531
And I would strongly encourage you to
not succumb to the anxiety that everyone

00:11:26.531 --> 00:11:30.825
else seems to have, but rather to
carefully go through the capability

00:11:30.825 --> 00:11:35.661
of the disruptions all around you and
figure out how you can solve any problem.

00:11:35.661 --> 00:11:37.475
Simplify your life in this case.

00:11:37.475 --> 00:11:39.080
Focus on the things that you care about.

00:11:39.080 --> 00:11:42.465
Family, relationships, changing the world,
making money, whatever it is.

00:11:42.465 --> 00:11:44.590
It's okay, but get focused on that.

00:11:44.590 --> 00:11:45.871
Don't be distracted.

00:11:45.871 --> 00:11:49.448
Most people that I know, especially in
your generation spend too much time on

00:11:49.448 --> 00:11:52.740
things which are distracting
rather than focusing.

00:11:52.740 --> 00:11:57.497
And often, there are things that
they really can't change anyway.

00:11:57.497 --> 00:12:01.144
Stop worrying about things that you can't
control and try to make your own luck.

00:12:01.144 --> 00:12:02.970
Now you say, how do I make my own luck?

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:05.170
Well, first,
you've been pretty lucky to get here.

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:09.131
Good answer and
I consider myself the same.

00:12:09.131 --> 00:12:12.467
I'll tell you the story of my
very close friend, Eric Lander.

00:12:12.467 --> 00:12:16.630
He was teaching, I guess he was
teaching at a business school and

00:12:16.630 --> 00:12:19.187
he thought, I want to do something new.

00:12:19.187 --> 00:12:22.641
And he decided to move to Boston,
because he figured that,

00:12:22.641 --> 00:12:26.649
that was the highest number of
interactions that would occur per unit

00:12:26.649 --> 00:12:29.130
of time in the areas that he cared about.

00:12:29.130 --> 00:12:30.963
He knew nothing about genetics.

00:12:30.963 --> 00:12:31.660
He shows up.

00:12:31.660 --> 00:12:32.436
He wanders around.

00:12:32.436 --> 00:12:36.903
He meets a faculty member who turns
out to be a brilliant geneticist and

00:12:36.903 --> 00:12:40.855
working with him,
becomes a brilliant geneticist himself.

00:12:40.855 --> 00:12:44.775
One of the most famous in the country,
but he had to move to Boston,

00:12:44.775 --> 00:12:50.250
because that's where the target was and
then he was willing to accept the risk.

00:12:50.250 --> 00:12:54.900
So sometimes life works that way that you
move there, but he made the luck happen.

00:12:56.040 --> 00:13:00.840
And in fact, when I say this,
remember that almost everything that

00:13:00.840 --> 00:13:03.580
happens to you happens,
because the people that you're with.

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:07.991
Every single success that I've had is
been, ciz I've been surrounded by these

00:13:07.991 --> 00:13:10.217
incredibly, interesting and
successful people.

00:13:10.217 --> 00:13:14.580
This technical person is a PhD in
my career and so forth, and so on.

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:17.500
But force yourself to take advantage
of all of this, don't just sit at home.

00:13:18.590 --> 00:13:19.680
Live your life to the fullest.

00:13:19.680 --> 00:13:21.760
The most successful people work hard and

00:13:21.760 --> 00:13:26.050
play hard which means you should not watch
television, you should watch YouTube.

00:13:27.310 --> 00:13:28.429
It's very clear.

00:13:28.429 --> 00:13:32.934
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This sort of change we can

00:13:32.934 --> 00:13:35.060
believe in is in fact, you.

00:13:35.060 --> 00:13:38.048
Your ability to adapt to
this environment around.

00:13:38.048 --> 00:13:44.369
I would also suggested that you develop at
least a fake charm, if you don't have any.

00:13:44.369 --> 00:13:45.750
Sorry.

00:13:45.750 --> 00:13:49.479
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Take a picture of someone's child and

00:13:49.479 --> 00:13:51.070
send it to the parent.

00:13:51.070 --> 00:13:54.416
Explain to someone's parent how
valuable their employee is.

00:13:54.416 --> 00:13:56.300
By the way, it works every time.

00:13:56.300 --> 00:13:57.720
Trust me.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:13:59.795
Don't say nice hairdo,
which is what I would say.

00:13:59.795 --> 00:14:03.370
Say, love your new hair and the
combination with that jewelry is sublime.

00:14:05.230 --> 00:14:07.468
That's fake charm, it works very well.

00:14:07.468 --> 00:14:11.751
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; When you're asked what your

00:14:11.751 --> 00:14:15.520
favorite number is, always say, 17.

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:18.018
It shows you understand prime numbers.

00:14:18.018 --> 00:14:22.332
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I mean, you need a leg up,

00:14:22.332 --> 00:14:23.310
you went to Stanford.

00:14:25.240 --> 00:14:28.409
The best prefix in a sentence
is the word imagine.

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:35.181
If I say imagine to you,
that means I'm talking to you.

00:14:35.181 --> 00:14:36.240
I'm getting you to think.

00:14:36.240 --> 00:14:40.453
I'm getting you to be
curious that you're hopeful.

00:14:40.453 --> 00:14:42.025
Seek meaning versus novelty.

00:14:42.025 --> 00:14:46.170
It's fun to have novelty, but meaning is
really how you make your life happen.

00:14:49.190 --> 00:14:53.260
As you create accomplishments, it's the
meaning and the impact that you have in

00:14:53.260 --> 00:14:57.430
the people that you work with which will
provide this incredible satisfaction.

00:14:58.740 --> 00:15:04.080
My sort of pet peeve with strategy is that
it's always taught in a short timeframe.

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:07.960
But in fact, the best strategies
have longer timeframe horizons.

00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:12.360
There's these traditional sayings,

00:15:12.360 --> 00:15:16.360
which I like which are we tend to
overestimate what can be done in 18 months

00:15:16.360 --> 00:15:18.490
and underestimate what
can be done in 10 years.

00:15:19.550 --> 00:15:21.500
Watch a movie from ten years ago or

00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:23.840
an interview in the technology
space from ten years ago and

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:28.220
see how much has changed, starting with
your mobile phone to make the point.

00:15:29.940 --> 00:15:33.889
But almost all of the strategies,
if you don't think about them right,

00:15:33.889 --> 00:15:35.780
fail within a five-year period.

00:15:35.780 --> 00:15:36.955
So, have a five-year plan.

00:15:36.955 --> 00:15:39.794
And of course, do it not just for
your business,

00:15:39.794 --> 00:15:41.889
but also for yourself personally.

00:15:41.889 --> 00:15:46.185
Be extraordinarily curious and don't
assume the status quo is unchangeable.

00:15:46.185 --> 00:15:51.100
I learned this from Larry and Sergey
who basically just eschewed tradition,

00:15:51.100 --> 00:15:55.650
because I was sort of a traditionalist and
what do we have today, publicly?

00:15:55.650 --> 00:15:58.375
We have Trump and Brexit,
all of these things.

00:15:58.375 --> 00:16:03.330
Almost all of the emerging tech
startups are essentially Disruptive

00:16:03.330 --> 00:16:08.320
in the economic sense and the political
sense and they were unexpected.

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:10.880
You should expect the unexpected
the unexpected as a result.

00:16:11.900 --> 00:16:16.370
And success then is determined by
persistence that is grit as well as

00:16:16.370 --> 00:16:19.420
curiosity and you have to combine both,
you have to be willing to do both.

00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:24.370
So the way to be happy then,
you put all this together,

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:29.150
is to have meaning in your life is
literally to serve others in what you do.

00:16:29.150 --> 00:16:31.460
Ultimately, that's the thing
that will matter the most.

00:16:31.460 --> 00:16:32.870
It's why I teach here.

00:16:32.870 --> 00:16:34.750
It's why many of you will come back and

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:37.930
honor the university
that you're a part of.

00:16:37.930 --> 00:16:42.150
But if I make a list I think
stay open to new things and

00:16:42.150 --> 00:16:46.030
unlock more happiness, be happy, surround
yourself with new things, new ideas,

00:16:46.030 --> 00:16:48.170
avoid pre-conception and
know more truths, right?

00:16:48.170 --> 00:16:50.654
Literally know things.

00:16:50.654 --> 00:16:54.889
But it seems to me that in order to
be disciplined, in order to get this.

00:16:54.889 --> 00:17:00.015
That is to have your own freedom, you have
to have enough self-discipline, right,

00:17:00.015 --> 00:17:05.190
and enough sort of focus, persistence,
if you will, to get what you want.

00:17:05.190 --> 00:17:08.935
So it's almost an oxymoron but it works.

00:17:08.935 --> 00:17:14.800
Now you sit there and you go at the end
of the day, what's you're proof, Eric?

00:17:14.800 --> 00:17:19.855
Well, poverty $1 a day changed 80%

00:17:19.855 --> 00:17:24.330
in my lifetime, plunged, right?

00:17:24.330 --> 00:17:28.320
So if you think that, I was sitting in
a meeting a couple days ago with somebody

00:17:28.320 --> 00:17:30.468
who said, things are terrible
in the developing world.

00:17:30.468 --> 00:17:32.890
Then I said,
they've always been terrible and

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:37.590
they're less terrible, right,
those are the facts, right?

00:17:37.590 --> 00:17:41.350
My father took pictures when
he was roughly you guys' age.

00:17:41.350 --> 00:17:44.940
He was traveling around Africa and you
look at the pictures of Africa then versus

00:17:44.940 --> 00:17:50.220
the Africa now, it's still poor but
it's a lot less poor.

00:17:50.220 --> 00:17:51.750
To the credit of
the people who built that.

00:17:53.930 --> 00:17:55.470
I think to finish with this and

00:17:55.470 --> 00:18:00.000
go to your questions I would say
I think you should recognize

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:04.230
that inequality is going to increase in
the world in our current economic system.

00:18:05.370 --> 00:18:09.410
And you need to be grateful that you're on
this side of the inequality dispersion.

00:18:11.000 --> 00:18:14.220
I don't think we know how to
solve that problem, as a society.

00:18:14.220 --> 00:18:15.620
And it's an important one to solve.

00:18:16.910 --> 00:18:20.190
It's a problem and
you're on the good side of that.

00:18:20.190 --> 00:18:21.120
Be grateful about it.

00:18:22.910 --> 00:18:25.710
And I also think that as
I said at the beginning,

00:18:25.710 --> 00:18:29.840
we seem to have lost this egalitarian
notion, at least in the media and

00:18:29.840 --> 00:18:33.414
in the politics of many countries now,
not just in America.

00:18:33.414 --> 00:18:36.839
I think you should recognize that
diversity is a strength, tolerance is

00:18:36.839 --> 00:18:40.500
a virtue, that most people are doing
what they think the world asks for them.

00:18:40.500 --> 00:18:45.750
And that the essential
human virtue is goodness.

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:51.440
Start there, and then look for the
exceptions, as opposed to the opposite.

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:54.790
And I'll finish by saying what I
say to everybody that I meet who

00:18:54.790 --> 00:18:59.660
ask me sort of how to organize themselves,
what I do is I tell them to say yes.

00:19:01.200 --> 00:19:02.740
Say yes to things.

00:19:02.740 --> 00:19:03.720
Just say yes, right?

00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:05.640
Just say yes, figure out a way to do it.

00:19:06.960 --> 00:19:08.330
Find a way to say yes to things.

00:19:08.330 --> 00:19:11.640
Say yes to invitations to a new country,
say yes to meet new friends,

00:19:11.640 --> 00:19:13.060
say yes to learn something new.

00:19:13.060 --> 00:19:17.970
Yes is how you get your first job, your
next job, your spouse, even your kids.

00:19:17.970 --> 00:19:20.680
Even if it's a bit edgy,
a bit out of your comfort zone, saying

00:19:20.680 --> 00:19:24.700
yes means that you'll do something new,
meet something new and make a difference.

00:19:24.700 --> 00:19:29.481
Yes let's you stand out on a crowd,
be the optimist, see the glass full,

00:19:29.481 --> 00:19:31.737
be the one that everyone comes to.

00:19:31.737 --> 00:19:33.254
Yes is what keeps us all young.

00:19:33.254 --> 00:19:37.640
So with that,

00:19:37.640 --> 00:19:44.250
thank you very much.

00:19:44.250 --> 00:19:47.337
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Let's see, now how are we going to do,

00:19:47.337 --> 00:19:48.510
you have some mics?

00:19:48.510 --> 00:19:50.594
Comments or questions, any topic is good.

00:19:50.594 --> 00:19:53.090
&gt;&gt; As we've talked about
before if you have a question,

00:19:53.090 --> 00:19:55.370
just please make yourself
known to Julie or Ali.

00:19:55.370 --> 00:20:00.940
And then, stand up, introduce yourself,
and ask Eric directly.

00:20:03.551 --> 00:20:07.876
&gt;&gt; Hi, Steve at MSX.

00:20:07.876 --> 00:20:10.612
I was interested in your
concept of abundance.

00:20:10.612 --> 00:20:13.428
And that obviously feels right.

00:20:13.428 --> 00:20:17.351
One of the interesting things is that
the societies,where this abundance is

00:20:17.351 --> 00:20:20.640
occurring do appear to be
becoming more unequal.

00:20:20.640 --> 00:20:24.970
And I wonder how you think about that from
a policy and technology point of view and

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:28.920
whether you think technology has any
role to play, I guess, in the future.

00:20:28.920 --> 00:20:30.428
And is this a risk to the world?

00:20:30.428 --> 00:20:33.370
&gt;&gt; Well, Jared Colon and
I wrote a book on this and

00:20:33.370 --> 00:20:36.080
we looked a lot at
the basis of connectivity.

00:20:37.360 --> 00:20:41.820
And my initial assumption was
that connectivity would be

00:20:41.820 --> 00:20:44.910
uniformly improving for
everyone in the world.

00:20:44.910 --> 00:20:47.640
So one of the stories
that we talk a lot about

00:20:47.640 --> 00:20:49.980
is the story of third world connectivity.

00:20:49.980 --> 00:20:53.320
That is, developing connectivity and
the power of very,

00:20:53.320 --> 00:20:55.530
very inexpensive smartphones.

00:20:55.530 --> 00:21:00.100
And one of the things that I am proudest
of, and Google and Android play a major,

00:21:00.100 --> 00:21:04.780
major role in this, is that that phone,
when it arrives in the village,

00:21:04.780 --> 00:21:08.380
is far more important than the arrival
of television a while earlier.

00:21:08.380 --> 00:21:12.700
Because it's a source of entertainment,
of information, of communication,

00:21:12.700 --> 00:21:15.320
and of safety, of photography,
and all of that.

00:21:16.370 --> 00:21:20.048
And it really has changed for
much for the better, and

00:21:20.048 --> 00:21:22.432
billions and billions of people.

00:21:22.432 --> 00:21:25.309
We're going to go from roughly three
billion people online to roughly five,

00:21:25.309 --> 00:21:26.324
because of those phones.

00:21:26.324 --> 00:21:29.323
But then we went back and we said, well,

00:21:29.323 --> 00:21:33.800
if that's such a great news,
right, what's the math?

00:21:33.800 --> 00:21:37.920
And the math is that while connectivity
for those people is getting much better,

00:21:39.300 --> 00:21:44.640
the bandwidth to the developed world
is increasing far greater, right?

00:21:44.640 --> 00:21:47.100
So if you take that
simple dialogue which is

00:21:48.690 --> 00:21:53.240
capitalism global trade improves life
at the bottom, but the people at

00:21:53.240 --> 00:21:57.420
the upper class are vastly more empowered
with something as powerful as bandwidth.

00:21:57.420 --> 00:21:59.670
That's a simple example of this.

00:21:59.670 --> 00:22:01.660
It's obviously true of many other things.

00:22:01.660 --> 00:22:05.695
So in industries where you have
declining costs which is communications,

00:22:05.695 --> 00:22:08.319
computer chips, silicon,
that kind of thing,

00:22:08.319 --> 00:22:11.790
I think the story is at least you
can get it everywhere as long as.

00:22:11.790 --> 00:22:13.970
And that's also going to be true of food.

00:22:13.970 --> 00:22:18.700
So remember food, a lot of a food
in the future will be essentially

00:22:20.830 --> 00:22:23.420
manufactured in new ways.

00:22:23.420 --> 00:22:25.910
Eventually, certainly in your lifetimes,

00:22:25.910 --> 00:22:29.910
some if not all of the meat you will eat
will have been developed from real meat.

00:22:29.910 --> 00:22:36.860
It'll be grown in the equivalent of
a culture, not an equivalent of a cow.

00:22:36.860 --> 00:22:38.818
And you sit there and say, well,
that sounds like a terrible idea.

00:22:38.818 --> 00:22:41.967
Well, it's going to have
a lot fewer diseases,

00:22:41.967 --> 00:22:46.746
it's going to be a lot healthier for
you, and people will work that out.

00:22:46.746 --> 00:22:51.259
So the economic answer is that you're
better off letting trade happen and

00:22:51.259 --> 00:22:53.930
letting this inequality occur.

00:22:53.930 --> 00:22:58.237
And then use redistribution as policies or
other mechanisms,

00:22:58.237 --> 00:23:03.125
right, to support the less fortunate,
and I'm roughly in that camp.

00:23:03.125 --> 00:23:06.332
I'm skeptical that if you
look in an aggregate basis,

00:23:06.332 --> 00:23:10.376
we're probably better off doing the trade,
doing the investment,

00:23:10.376 --> 00:23:13.320
creating the companies,
allowing it to become.

00:23:13.320 --> 00:23:17.746
And then making the difference with
policies of one car another and you can do

00:23:17.746 --> 00:23:22.470
this with minimum wages, you can do this
with various other forms of subsidies.

00:23:23.640 --> 00:23:30.506
Lots and lots of benefits that
are needed to make up for the gap.

00:23:30.506 --> 00:23:31.928
I should say, by the way,

00:23:31.928 --> 00:23:35.380
that what I just said is completely
politically unpalatable.

00:23:35.380 --> 00:23:38.981
Because of the hollowing out
that's going on in rural and

00:23:38.981 --> 00:23:41.540
manufacturing areas around the world.

00:23:41.540 --> 00:23:47.080
For example, if you go in France, Paris
is very dynamic as it always has been.

00:23:47.080 --> 00:23:51.714
But there's a job problem,
even in a socialist country,

00:23:51.714 --> 00:23:55.222
outside of it because
of all these changes.

00:23:55.222 --> 00:23:57.215
&gt;&gt; Hello, my name is Patrick Daniel.

00:23:57.215 --> 00:23:58.354
Thanks so much for sharing your insights.

00:23:58.354 --> 00:23:59.486
I really appreciate it.

00:23:59.486 --> 00:24:06.243
So I was wondering to hear your
thoughts on sort of the revolution

00:24:06.243 --> 00:24:12.180
that's happening with blockchain
technology and decentralized networks.

00:24:12.180 --> 00:24:16.855
I'm really curious to understand how 200
years from now, when people look back at

00:24:16.855 --> 00:24:21.765
our time, and the big turning points that
will redefine society, our civilization.

00:24:22.895 --> 00:24:25.895
If we think about it, all these things are
happening, you highlighted some of them.

00:24:27.055 --> 00:24:28.533
And I'm just wondering,

00:24:28.533 --> 00:24:33.170
how you think about this whole clash
between hierarchies, centralization, and

00:24:33.170 --> 00:24:36.680
decentralization, and
you know all these stuff is going on?

00:24:36.680 --> 00:24:39.063
Because as Edmund Burke,
the historian, said,

00:24:39.063 --> 00:24:41.710
we have to have a contract
between generations.

00:24:41.710 --> 00:24:44.340
And a lot of people don't
understand blockchain technology is

00:24:44.340 --> 00:24:45.970
not very intuitive.

00:24:45.970 --> 00:24:49.120
So I'm just curious to understand
how you see all these different

00:24:49.120 --> 00:24:51.630
decentralized trends and
distribute networks and

00:24:51.630 --> 00:24:55.920
how this affects society,
because it's happening right now.

00:24:55.920 --> 00:24:58.410
&gt;&gt; Your question is exactly
at the center of what

00:24:58.410 --> 00:25:01.620
I mentioned was I thought
the dichotomy on this issue.

00:25:01.620 --> 00:25:02.750
Because on the one hand,

00:25:02.750 --> 00:25:06.790
we're putting enormous power
in the hands of individuals.

00:25:06.790 --> 00:25:07.880
And at the same time,

00:25:07.880 --> 00:25:12.560
we're terrified at the result of that,
in some cases by overreacting.

00:25:12.560 --> 00:25:17.390
The specifics about blockchain are pretty
straightforward, which is that the ledger

00:25:17.390 --> 00:25:22.870
that's part of blockchain is likely to be
a fundamental computer science platform.

00:25:22.870 --> 00:25:27.950
The fact that you can have distributed
ledgers that accurately keep information

00:25:27.950 --> 00:25:35.170
that's not copyable or fungible, if you
will, is a major computer science advance.

00:25:35.170 --> 00:25:39.363
But if you look, all of the great
optimists about blockchain and

00:25:39.363 --> 00:25:43.700
Bitcoin have been stymied by
the historic structures of banking,

00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:47.600
which were erected after
enormous financial failures.

00:25:47.600 --> 00:25:50.740
And they're not likely to be very liberal,
right?

00:25:50.740 --> 00:25:53.780
They didn't hire liberals to do this,
by definition.

00:25:53.780 --> 00:25:56.380
The liberals were the ones who
got them in trouble 50 years ago.

00:25:56.380 --> 00:26:00.290
You asked a question about 200 years, and

00:26:00.290 --> 00:26:04.600
I think here is a build on how
200 years might look like.

00:26:06.380 --> 00:26:09.540
For the next five to ten years,

00:26:09.540 --> 00:26:15.930
the AI stuff that I'm talking about
is incredibly, obviously beneficial.

00:26:15.930 --> 00:26:19.140
So I mentioned healthcare,
I mentioned drug discovery,

00:26:19.140 --> 00:26:23.060
I mentioned basic science,
transportation, these kind of things.

00:26:24.170 --> 00:26:25.420
And it's happening so

00:26:25.420 --> 00:26:29.843
quickly, I don't think people understand
how quickly our lives will change.

00:26:29.843 --> 00:26:31.827
In the same sense that ten years ago,

00:26:31.827 --> 00:26:37.440
remember the iPhone came out in roughly
2007, so that was roughly ten years ago.

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:41.260
I don't think people understood the impact
of having such a powerful super computer

00:26:41.260 --> 00:26:42.270
on your hand would have.

00:26:42.270 --> 00:26:43.340
I think it'll be a similar thing.

00:26:44.600 --> 00:26:49.496
What happens after that, a lot people in
my community believed that we're going to

00:26:49.496 --> 00:26:53.528
be able to get to the point where
computers can have aspects of human

00:26:53.528 --> 00:26:56.485
intelligent that look like intuition,
right.

00:26:56.485 --> 00:26:57.444
That they have memory, and

00:26:57.444 --> 00:26:59.730
that they have the ability to
do the equivalent of dreaming.

00:26:59.730 --> 00:27:03.570
I don't mean soul like dreaming,
I mean dreaming in the sense of imagining

00:27:03.570 --> 00:27:05.710
scenarios and
putting interesting things together.

00:27:06.740 --> 00:27:10.940
If that's true, then at some point,
perhaps in my lifetime,

00:27:10.940 --> 00:27:13.590
it should be possible to feed,
for example,

00:27:13.590 --> 00:27:18.050
all of math into this computer and all of
physics into this computer, and behold,

00:27:18.050 --> 00:27:22.820
some new discoveries and
some real effect on timing.

00:27:24.390 --> 00:27:28.770
It gets harder to see what
happens after that, right?

00:27:28.770 --> 00:27:32.940
How does this emergent technology
evolve in a 50 year period?

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:37.831
If you'd said, I mentioned that the
computer that I used, which was 40 years

00:27:37.831 --> 00:27:43.230
ago, is a 100 million times slower then
the one that is in it's place today.

00:27:43.230 --> 00:27:47.900
Very difficult to imagine 50
years from now what that can be.

00:27:47.900 --> 00:27:52.479
Eventually, you have to assume, that there
will be, the brain is a biological moment

00:27:52.479 --> 00:27:55.993
and eventually the brain will be much,
much better understood and

00:27:55.993 --> 00:28:00.447
there'll be greater connection between
the brain and then these digital systems,

00:28:00.447 --> 00:28:04.054
exactly how that will be achieved,
is a matter of science fiction.

00:28:06.540 --> 00:28:12.249
Question over there, yes sir?

00:28:12.249 --> 00:28:13.404
&gt;&gt; Hi [INAUDIBLE] MBA one.

00:28:13.404 --> 00:28:15.964
Somehow related to
the the previous question,

00:28:15.964 --> 00:28:19.111
what do you believe are the biggest
mega trends right now?

00:28:19.111 --> 00:28:23.409
And especially, what do you believe we
should focus on, on which trends we should

00:28:23.409 --> 00:28:26.524
focus on, especially if we want
to start our own startup and

00:28:26.524 --> 00:28:29.000
get on the tail wind of those mega trends?

00:28:29.000 --> 00:28:30.990
Thank you.
&gt;&gt; If I were a computer scientist at

00:28:30.990 --> 00:28:35.130
your age, I would work on systems AI,
because that's, I think,

00:28:35.130 --> 00:28:38.530
where all the real power's going to be,
which is what I've been talking about.

00:28:38.530 --> 00:28:40.895
I'll give you an example in AI.

00:28:40.895 --> 00:28:44.959
One of the problems with the models that
have been generated is they can't figure

00:28:44.959 --> 00:28:49.770
out exactly how they work, they work,
but they don't exactly understand.

00:28:49.770 --> 00:28:54.768
And so, there's research now in both,
called attributive models, where they

00:28:54.768 --> 00:28:59.324
can actually attribute a decision that
was made to how the model learned,

00:28:59.324 --> 00:29:01.621
because the systems are about training.

00:29:01.621 --> 00:29:05.642
One of the bizarre things about these
systems is that you don't program them, so

00:29:05.642 --> 00:29:08.717
if they come up with the wrong answer,
you can't just go in and

00:29:08.717 --> 00:29:11.338
change a little bit of code,
you have to retrain it.

00:29:11.338 --> 00:29:13.170
And that's an important example.

00:29:15.910 --> 00:29:19.341
My favorite example there is,
you're in the self driving car, and

00:29:19.341 --> 00:29:22.560
the self driving car has
been taught by the firm.

00:29:22.560 --> 00:29:24.300
And it learned what humans do,

00:29:24.300 --> 00:29:28.590
which is it sort of did
a rolling stop at the stop sign.

00:29:28.590 --> 00:29:32.260
And the policeman sees you and
pulls you over, and

00:29:32.260 --> 00:29:35.990
the policeman says,
you didn't stop at the stop sign.

00:29:35.990 --> 00:29:40.420
And you say correctly, officer I
was not driving, it's not my fault.

00:29:40.420 --> 00:29:43.200
And the officer says,
who is driving the car?

00:29:43.200 --> 00:29:46.450
And the car says, I was driving.

00:29:46.450 --> 00:29:50.050
And the officer says,
why did you run the stop sign?

00:29:50.050 --> 00:29:51.320
And the car says, I don't know.

00:29:52.470 --> 00:29:54.000
And the car says, who do I arrest now?

00:29:55.580 --> 00:29:58.120
And every player is
truthful in that scenario.

00:29:59.130 --> 00:30:00.360
These problems will get solved.

00:30:02.430 --> 00:30:05.930
If I were not a computer scientist,
I'd be a biologist,

00:30:05.930 --> 00:30:11.230
because taking the analog world
of biology to the digital world,

00:30:11.230 --> 00:30:14.790
means answers that have bedeviled
humanity for a thousand years.

00:30:16.020 --> 00:30:20.680
And so, that says you can make money
in those two areas, literally,

00:30:20.680 --> 00:30:22.320
in the next five years.

00:30:22.320 --> 00:30:28.580
And the rate of investment is at a scale
I've not seen in a decade, maybe 20 years.

00:30:29.650 --> 00:30:32.560
Literally, we're taking mathematicians and
physicists, who are brilliant, and

00:30:32.560 --> 00:30:36.060
otherwise unemployed, and
retraining them on this stuff.

00:30:36.060 --> 00:30:37.370
That's how powerful it really is.

00:30:40.430 --> 00:30:40.990
More questions?

00:30:40.990 --> 00:30:41.840
Yes sir.
&gt;&gt; I'm Paul Needham, I'm MBA too,

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:45.830
and to pick up on what you talked
about in your comments earlier.

00:30:45.830 --> 00:30:50.150
With the question of really big companies,
and obviously we've all read

00:30:50.150 --> 00:30:54.270
in the papers recently about monopoly
questions, and antitrust questions.

00:30:54.270 --> 00:30:56.680
And obviously,
you don't think Google is a monopoly,

00:30:56.680 --> 00:31:00.790
but when would you start to
wonder about any company?

00:31:00.790 --> 00:31:05.240
If antitrust intervention is necessary,
what are the signs you would be looking

00:31:05.240 --> 00:31:11.380
for where you would worry about
monopoly power as a general matter?

00:31:11.380 --> 00:31:14.300
&gt;&gt; I, obviously, know a lot about this,
because I fought Microsoft, and

00:31:14.300 --> 00:31:17.020
I've also testified on this, and spent
quite a bit of time with the European

00:31:17.020 --> 00:31:18.616
government with respect
to the Google activities.

00:31:18.616 --> 00:31:23.025
The U.S. answer is that,
which came out of the Rockefeller age, so

00:31:23.025 --> 00:31:25.925
a 100 years ago, the standard is not set.

00:31:25.925 --> 00:31:29.453
But basically,
whenever you're doing things,

00:31:29.453 --> 00:31:34.997
which are anticompetitive, which do not
allow entrants to enter the market,

00:31:34.997 --> 00:31:38.273
and certainly,
when I dealt with Microsoft,

00:31:38.273 --> 00:31:43.406
the problem was Microsoft owned
the channel, owned the distribution.

00:31:43.406 --> 00:31:46.570
And they were the only place where
the apps could be distributed.

00:31:46.570 --> 00:31:52.490
Whereas the Internet has essentially
eliminated that control over distribution.

00:31:52.490 --> 00:31:55.790
So the good news about
the internet is that, and

00:31:55.790 --> 00:32:00.510
again, we just published a version of
a book, our latest book, how Google works,

00:32:00.510 --> 00:32:02.285
which talks a lot about this.

00:32:02.285 --> 00:32:03.462
Is that the Internet is so

00:32:03.462 --> 00:32:06.321
competitive that your product
really does have to be good and

00:32:06.321 --> 00:32:10.038
if it's really good, you can raise
the money, you can get the distribution.

00:32:10.038 --> 00:32:13.098
I just don't see the barriers to entry on,

00:32:13.098 --> 00:32:16.845
at least on the Internet side,
at least for awhile.

00:32:16.845 --> 00:32:21.533
And I think what I would say is if you
look today, there are depending on how you

00:32:21.533 --> 00:32:25.373
count it four or five companies
that are defining most of this.

00:32:25.373 --> 00:32:31.705
And they are Apple, Google, Amazon,
Microsoft, Facebook and that's roughly

00:32:31.705 --> 00:32:38.870
the order of the top five corporations
in the American stock market evaluation.

00:32:38.870 --> 00:32:44.150
So we're up to something in the sense that
this is working they're global businesses,

00:32:44.150 --> 00:32:46.820
the competition is brutal and
you get the benefit of that.

00:32:46.820 --> 00:32:49.334
So the reason that your iPhone or
Android phone is so

00:32:49.334 --> 00:32:53.718
incredibly powerful is not because we're
so wonderful, it's because the competition

00:32:53.718 --> 00:32:56.947
is the most brutal competition
I've seen since the PC industry.

00:32:56.947 --> 00:32:59.301
Maybe even more so.

00:32:59.301 --> 00:33:00.797
That would be the way I
would sort of answer it.

00:33:03.332 --> 00:33:04.826
Yes sir.

00:33:04.826 --> 00:33:07.662
&gt;&gt; Hi, Nat Gardenswartz, MBA One.

00:33:07.662 --> 00:33:09.020
Thank you so much for coming.

00:33:09.020 --> 00:33:13.040
Would you be willing to comment on
the relationship as you see it between

00:33:13.040 --> 00:33:17.194
the science and technology communities
on the one hand and government and

00:33:17.194 --> 00:33:18.542
politics on the other?

00:33:18.542 --> 00:33:23.219
And what are some ways you might envision
the two different sort of parties,

00:33:23.219 --> 00:33:27.622
perhaps engaging more constructively
with each other going forward?

00:33:27.622 --> 00:33:30.315
&gt;&gt; Well, it's not getting better,
it's getting worse.

00:33:30.315 --> 00:33:36.265
A little bit about the history is that the
NIH funding, which funds the research for

00:33:36.265 --> 00:33:42.070
medical stuff in the United States,
peaked around 2008, 2009-ish.

00:33:42.070 --> 00:33:47.117
It fell roughly 30% in real
dollars between then and

00:33:47.117 --> 00:33:50.638
the end of the Obama administration.

00:33:50.638 --> 00:33:56.707
Non-medical research has been
roughly flat, as a percentage.

00:33:56.707 --> 00:34:00.184
And now the new Trump proposal is
an evisceration of those numbers,

00:34:00.184 --> 00:34:03.670
and a significant reduction and
furthermore at NIH.

00:34:03.670 --> 00:34:05.004
Why you would want to,

00:34:05.004 --> 00:34:08.949
speaking as a person with some
self-interest into my own health.

00:34:08.949 --> 00:34:13.366
Why you would want to decrease the amount
of investment in health research when you

00:34:13.366 --> 00:34:17.912
just have invented the technologies like
Krisper that will cause the breakthroughs

00:34:17.912 --> 00:34:20.728
that will solve these problems
that have existed for

00:34:20.728 --> 00:34:23.880
thousands of years in
humanity is beyond me.

00:34:23.880 --> 00:34:28.865
Right, the only policy stupider is the H1B
policy, which is another discussion.

00:34:28.865 --> 00:34:30.260
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; All right,

00:34:30.260 --> 00:34:31.425
I don't need to tell you about that one.

00:34:31.425 --> 00:34:36.230
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; So,

00:34:37.290 --> 00:34:43.949
there's some weird mix of nativism,
lack of understanding, scientific bias.

00:34:45.270 --> 00:34:49.060
So I'm not sure how to solve this problem
but I would go thru some things like this.

00:34:49.060 --> 00:34:54.030
For science is true, right and
while there are debates

00:34:54.030 --> 00:34:58.430
within scientists, the fact of the matter
is the fact you have to prove stuff and

00:34:58.430 --> 00:35:01.740
that your constantly being
falsified keeps it roughly right.

00:35:01.740 --> 00:35:04.708
Climate change by the way is also true,
right?

00:35:04.708 --> 00:35:07.090
It's actually happening, right?

00:35:07.090 --> 00:35:08.115
It's a fact.

00:35:08.115 --> 00:35:10.663
You can choose to ignore it, right?

00:35:10.663 --> 00:35:14.039
A reasonable strategy for
the Luddites, I got it, okay.

00:35:14.039 --> 00:35:16.447
But don't deny it, right?

00:35:16.447 --> 00:35:19.212
And don't be a denier of basic facts.

00:35:19.212 --> 00:35:22.450
I mean, I love these people that say
well we're operating in a matrix.

00:35:22.450 --> 00:35:24.660
Okay, well good, okay take a knife.

00:35:24.660 --> 00:35:28.135
Put your hand out and put the knife
into your hand and tell me if it hurts.

00:35:28.135 --> 00:35:30.700
Okay, does it matter whether
you're in the matrix or not?

00:35:30.700 --> 00:35:32.793
It still hurts, right?

00:35:32.793 --> 00:35:34.140
So let's deal with some facts.

00:35:36.657 --> 00:35:40.563
So, there's a community of people,
including myself,

00:35:40.563 --> 00:35:44.947
who are working hard to try to get
these messages through to people

00:35:44.947 --> 00:35:50.890
who spend their time denying science for
political or material reasons.

00:35:50.890 --> 00:35:52.580
A simple rule is that,

00:35:52.580 --> 00:35:56.920
often special interests in our system
are paying them to believe falsehoods.

00:35:58.550 --> 00:36:00.170
That's a bad way to run a country.

00:36:00.170 --> 00:36:01.750
It's a bad way to run it forever.

00:36:01.750 --> 00:36:02.870
It's not a new problem.

00:36:02.870 --> 00:36:03.980
It's gotten worse.

00:36:03.980 --> 00:36:05.600
So we need to fight it, and
we need to fight it hard.

00:36:06.780 --> 00:36:10.455
There are proposals to cut,
I can go on, but I'll stop.

00:36:10.455 --> 00:36:14.551
There are proposals to cut basic research
funding that goes to universities

00:36:14.551 --> 00:36:16.733
including to this university, right?

00:36:16.733 --> 00:36:20.760
Think about the value of the product
that is produced by this university.

00:36:20.760 --> 00:36:22.866
You shouldn't cut it,
you should increase it.

00:36:26.208 --> 00:36:30.467
[INAUDIBLE]
&gt;&gt; Hi, originally from Israel.

00:36:30.467 --> 00:36:34.948
I wanted to get your take on how do we
close the gender gap in the business and

00:36:34.948 --> 00:36:35.755
tech world?

00:36:39.552 --> 00:36:42.358
&gt;&gt; Bill has worked hard on this.

00:36:42.358 --> 00:36:47.682
And if you go through
this like three phases.

00:36:47.682 --> 00:36:51.229
So the first phase is the traditional
prejudices, sort of male-only world.

00:36:51.229 --> 00:36:53.607
And again,
watch a movie from 50 years ago.

00:36:53.607 --> 00:36:55.070
You'll see all those patterns.

00:36:55.070 --> 00:36:57.258
I think those have largely been addressed.

00:36:57.258 --> 00:37:01.864
The second one was trying
to get employment systems

00:37:01.864 --> 00:37:05.515
that would work for men as well as women.

00:37:05.515 --> 00:37:09.454
And that especially involves focusing
on child care, equality promotion,

00:37:09.454 --> 00:37:11.270
that sort of stuff.

00:37:11.270 --> 00:37:15.053
And I think recently, we figured out even
to deal with some of the pay gaps issues.

00:37:15.053 --> 00:37:17.096
One of the secrets of what we do,

00:37:17.096 --> 00:37:20.973
a story from ten years ago is
that when we interviewed women.

00:37:20.973 --> 00:37:25.832
A good example of pay gap,
we have studied this exhaustively.

00:37:25.832 --> 00:37:29.682
And the way you solve the pay gap, since
women come in with historical salaries

00:37:29.682 --> 00:37:33.720
lower than the men, is don't base your
salaries based on the historical salary.

00:37:33.720 --> 00:37:37.680
And if you follow that as a basic
principle, you will solve for

00:37:37.680 --> 00:37:40.780
the compensation issue, which we
do indeed believe we have done and

00:37:40.780 --> 00:37:42.160
we've issued lots of reports on that.

00:37:43.380 --> 00:37:47.688
The unconscious bias thing which everyone
is focusing on now is very real.

00:37:47.688 --> 00:37:51.536
If you talk to women they all say,
I feel like I'm not heard.

00:37:51.536 --> 00:37:52.725
I feel like I'm interrupted.

00:37:52.725 --> 00:37:54.765
I feel like I'm talked over.

00:37:54.765 --> 00:37:57.370
And corporations are now
beginning to include training,

00:37:57.370 --> 00:38:00.235
there´s no evidence that
the training is working yet.

00:38:00.235 --> 00:38:03.059
But maybe these people have to go
through multiple such trainings.

00:38:03.059 --> 00:38:08.201
But I would say to you it's smart to try
to have every conceivably talented person

00:38:08.201 --> 00:38:13.117
in your company, including all the men,
all the women, all the gay people,

00:38:13.117 --> 00:38:16.974
all the lesbians, all the Russians,
all the crazy people,

00:38:16.974 --> 00:38:20.859
all the people who are like
slightly insufferable, right?

00:38:20.859 --> 00:38:22.956
That you're kind of annoying to you,
right?

00:38:22.956 --> 00:38:27.597
If they really are the best in the world,
you need them in your company.

00:38:27.597 --> 00:38:32.474
Which is why the H-1B policy is so
stupid and why the tech industry is so

00:38:32.474 --> 00:38:35.809
unified on this question
about both equality but

00:38:35.809 --> 00:38:39.330
also the egalitarian and
openness that we aspire.

00:38:40.510 --> 00:38:45.216
What I've discovered since I've been
shocked by it, I'm always shocked by this,

00:38:45.216 --> 00:38:46.939
I've been doing this so long,

00:38:46.939 --> 00:38:50.073
that American firms are mostly
not like the tech firms.

00:38:50.073 --> 00:38:55.595
I can't speak for Israel,
although I think Israel, my wife and

00:38:55.595 --> 00:39:00.708
I did a fund to promote female
scientists in Israel to try and

00:39:00.708 --> 00:39:06.437
get them the funding for
that crucial period when they graduate and

00:39:06.437 --> 00:39:08.603
before they get tenure.

00:39:08.603 --> 00:39:10.240
So we're trying experiments like that.

00:39:12.210 --> 00:39:19.789
I think this is a solvable problem
More questions, over here, yes sir?

00:39:19.789 --> 00:39:22.001
Yes ma'am, excuse me.

00:39:22.001 --> 00:39:23.277
&gt;&gt; Hi, I'm Kelsey Page.

00:39:23.277 --> 00:39:24.493
I'm actually an undergrad.

00:39:24.493 --> 00:39:27.319
You talked a little bit
about falsehoods earlier.

00:39:27.319 --> 00:39:31.948
And my question is especially
considering the recent election and

00:39:31.948 --> 00:39:37.733
Google having so much influence over the
information people see on the Internet and

00:39:37.733 --> 00:39:41.864
how they get that information,
what do you see as Google's

00:39:41.864 --> 00:39:46.324
role in maybe social responsibility
in this like in this realm,

00:39:46.324 --> 00:39:50.899
and elaborating more on how you
see Google fighting falsehoods?

00:39:50.899 --> 00:39:55.333
&gt;&gt; So, there's sort of two
areas that we're focused on.

00:39:55.333 --> 00:39:57.909
The first is improving our ranking.

00:39:57.909 --> 00:40:01.780
I want to say to you right
upfront that Google is full,

00:40:01.780 --> 00:40:06.730
Of a number of false statements,
that are not on the first page, alright.

00:40:06.730 --> 00:40:10.650
Our job is to rank, and
our algorithms are pretty good

00:40:10.650 --> 00:40:15.182
at getting the truly idiotic-crazy
people who have no life,

00:40:15.182 --> 00:40:19.888
and ranking them below sort of
the more legitimate debates, and

00:40:19.888 --> 00:40:24.447
I'm very proud of that, and
that only gets better in my view.

00:40:24.447 --> 00:40:27.626
The second is that we have
a team called Jigsaw,

00:40:27.626 --> 00:40:32.636
which is working very hard to use
advances in language and understanding,

00:40:32.636 --> 00:40:36.611
to look for threatening speech,
and hate speech which is,

00:40:36.611 --> 00:40:41.805
if you familiar with Gresham's law,
that bad speech transact good speech.

00:40:41.805 --> 00:40:46.926
A lot of these communities are full
of people who just pile on in

00:40:46.926 --> 00:40:53.650
very distressing ways, simple and
it's gotten worse because of automation.

00:40:53.650 --> 00:40:58.850
So an example is, I give you a stereotype,
you have a woman who is very thoughtful,

00:40:58.850 --> 00:41:03.460
and she issues an impassionate
statement on a social media, and

00:41:03.460 --> 00:41:09.040
she is viciously attacked
in personal sexist,

00:41:09.040 --> 00:41:11.890
threatened in ways that
are just terrifying, and so

00:41:11.890 --> 00:41:16.220
she has lunch with her girlfriend
after this, and she says well frankly,

00:41:16.220 --> 00:41:19.530
I just didn't know there were
that many evil men in the world.

00:41:20.660 --> 00:41:23.430
And her girlfriend correctly says,
actually they weren't men,

00:41:23.430 --> 00:41:26.670
they were bots, right, that those attacks,

00:41:26.670 --> 00:41:31.470
that sort of relentless attack that you've
got, the suppression of your speech,

00:41:31.470 --> 00:41:35.770
was organized by evil people who
were using automation to do so.

00:41:35.770 --> 00:41:39.730
I think we can detect that,
with some work, so

00:41:39.730 --> 00:41:44.500
I don't think we're ever going to be able
to detect a legitimate group over here,

00:41:44.500 --> 00:41:48.540
versus legitimate group here that
has a disagreement, alright.

00:41:48.540 --> 00:41:53.770
That's a matter for God, and judges,
and politics, and so forth to sort out,

00:41:53.770 --> 00:41:58.810
but if it's a bad person over here,
writes something,

00:41:58.810 --> 00:42:02.440
this group takes it, automates it,
makes it look like a huge phenomena,

00:42:02.440 --> 00:42:04.790
press person over here
writes a huge new phenomena,

00:42:04.790 --> 00:42:07.615
which serves the interests of no one,
I think we can detect that.

00:42:07.615 --> 00:42:12.320
And that would be a big improvement,
and if we manage to pull it off,

00:42:12.320 --> 00:42:15.160
which I hope we will, as an industry now,

00:42:15.160 --> 00:42:19.200
it will arrive just in time as the evil
side will become more automated.

00:42:20.550 --> 00:42:23.908
So, if you look at the Russian
attack on the election,

00:42:23.908 --> 00:42:28.946
which appears to have been done by humans
not by computers, or we can debate that,

00:42:28.946 --> 00:42:33.838
imagine four years from now, with the
power of the tools that I'm describing,

00:42:33.838 --> 00:42:37.928
weaponizing that, right, and
making your voting participation

00:42:37.928 --> 00:42:42.163
your confidence in the process less so,
so your less likely to vote,

00:42:42.163 --> 00:42:46.948
less likely to express your opinions
as a citizen, that's not a good thing.

00:42:50.604 --> 00:42:51.700
&gt;&gt; My name is Ben.

00:42:51.700 --> 00:42:56.106
Thanks for coming in to speak today,
you had mentioned earlier on some

00:42:56.106 --> 00:43:00.570
comments around focusing in your career,
and related to that,

00:43:00.570 --> 00:43:05.310
I'd like to ask how you think
about your role now, and

00:43:05.310 --> 00:43:11.100
your role in the future, and ensuring that
technology is human centered, and instead

00:43:11.100 --> 00:43:15.550
of trying to create behavior change around
humans, that's technology centered.

00:43:18.810 --> 00:43:21.200
&gt;&gt; I can talk about my
personnel thoughts on this,

00:43:21.200 --> 00:43:25.110
I think that the businesses
that tech represents,

00:43:25.110 --> 00:43:29.770
are all working very hard to earn your
trust, and earn your attention, and

00:43:29.770 --> 00:43:34.830
keep you involved, and to monetize that
in some way, that's why everyone's

00:43:34.830 --> 00:43:39.090
addicted to all this stuff, I think that
will continue in one form or another.

00:43:40.760 --> 00:43:46.080
If you imagine the following scenario,
imagine we're so

00:43:46.080 --> 00:43:49.360
connected that we're all using these
knowledge networks that are so

00:43:49.360 --> 00:43:53.500
smart, that they help us every day,
which is part of our goal.

00:43:53.500 --> 00:43:58.830
There's also a possibility that those
knowledge networks can enforce a certain

00:43:58.830 --> 00:44:03.930
uniformity of view or a common view,
right, in the same role

00:44:03.930 --> 00:44:09.580
that the three networks had when I was
a young boy growing up in television, and

00:44:09.580 --> 00:44:13.530
our principles need to be independent
views, independent access,

00:44:13.530 --> 00:44:17.710
independent voices, and not suppression
of it, and I worry that this adoption of

00:44:17.710 --> 00:44:24.100
these platforms could
lead to a commonality

00:44:24.100 --> 00:44:29.800
of view than looking for divergent views,
and listening for the curious new voices.

00:44:29.800 --> 00:44:34.250
And I worry a lot about that because I
think the logic around the platforms,

00:44:34.250 --> 00:44:37.980
means we want everyone in one place,
and by definition any such platform has

00:44:37.980 --> 00:44:42.910
it's own language and it's own structure,
and humans are more creative than that.

00:44:42.910 --> 00:44:47.491
So, and the answer, of course is not
regulation, but more competition,

00:44:47.491 --> 00:44:50.983
more start-ups,
more ways of expressing yourself, so

00:44:50.983 --> 00:44:54.401
to me this next generation of AI and
knowledge systems,

00:44:54.401 --> 00:44:58.476
will take advantage of this and
produce new amazing insights, and

00:44:58.476 --> 00:45:02.879
will provide some competition for
the incumbents, including for us.

00:45:02.879 --> 00:45:05.389
Yes, sir?

00:45:05.389 --> 00:45:07.309
&gt;&gt; Hi, James Phillipe,
I met you [CROSSTALK]

00:45:07.309 --> 00:45:08.190
&gt;&gt; Nice to see you again.

00:45:09.710 --> 00:45:14.330
&gt;&gt; So, another article I read this
weekend was about yourself, and

00:45:14.330 --> 00:45:16.410
how Larry and Sergey,
when they were bringing you on,

00:45:17.570 --> 00:45:23.850
really cared about someone who could
foster group flow in an organization,

00:45:23.850 --> 00:45:26.750
so they took you to Burning Man,
to see whether you had it.

00:45:26.750 --> 00:45:28.895
&gt;&gt; That article is an excellent article,

00:45:28.895 --> 00:45:31.888
I suggest you not believe
everything you read online.

00:45:31.888 --> 00:45:35.793
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; I'm pleased at this point to hear it's

00:45:35.793 --> 00:45:39.622
fake news, but my question was really,
when we are making important

00:45:39.622 --> 00:45:44.111
people decisions in our life, whether
it's a senior hire, backing a founder,

00:45:44.111 --> 00:45:48.600
choosing the romantic partner to spend
your life with, what capabilities do you

00:45:48.600 --> 00:45:52.262
look for, and where would you take
them to see if they've got it?

00:45:52.262 --> 00:45:55.741
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Well I strongly suggest
you go to Burning Man then.

00:45:55.741 --> 00:45:58.953
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So, I had been going before, and

00:45:58.953 --> 00:46:04.201
they had also gone, and so we found
a kindred spirit there, and that's how

00:46:04.201 --> 00:46:09.560
it really happened, but I enjoy the false
narrative as much as anyone else.

00:46:11.430 --> 00:46:15.710
There are people who have
extraordinary skill in picking other

00:46:15.710 --> 00:46:20.390
people, and I would suggest that
that's something the Dean and I,

00:46:20.390 --> 00:46:25.870
were talking about things that could be
added to the curriculum and so forth, and

00:46:25.870 --> 00:46:30.250
the judgement of this is the person
I'm going to invest with,

00:46:30.250 --> 00:46:34.750
and so forth, is not a simple one, right?

00:46:34.750 --> 00:46:37.100
So, you need to be thorough,
but you need to be excited,

00:46:37.100 --> 00:46:41.090
you need to sort of fall in love, as well
as be practical, just all of those things,

00:46:41.090 --> 00:46:43.210
I don't think that's a solved problem.

00:46:43.210 --> 00:46:46.877
Well, I'll tell you what I do, and
what we did at Google, is first we

00:46:46.877 --> 00:46:51.053
wanted people who did more than one thing,
so we wanted them to be curious, and

00:46:51.053 --> 00:46:54.330
we wanted them to be
accomplished in something.

00:46:54.330 --> 00:46:58.250
So, Sergey's position was we should hire
every person who was a rocket scientist

00:46:58.250 --> 00:47:02.960
we could find, because they were
clearly oddballs, and any and

00:47:02.960 --> 00:47:08.140
all of our salespeople
should be Olympics winners,

00:47:09.420 --> 00:47:12.880
and I said okay, well that's
an interesting approach to sales.

00:47:12.880 --> 00:47:14.880
And he argued correctly,

00:47:14.880 --> 00:47:18.800
that somebody who can win
the Olympics has enormous discipline,

00:47:18.800 --> 00:47:23.100
think about what it takes to do what they
did, he was very impressed with them.

00:47:24.690 --> 00:47:27.630
So, what I learned was,
when you interviewed people,

00:47:27.630 --> 00:47:33.160
find out what they do that's not work
related, and see if it's compelling,

00:47:33.160 --> 00:47:37.620
if it's insightful,
tell me something I don't already know.

00:47:37.620 --> 00:47:42.310
That usually produces a very good
candidate, and it's a pretty hard test,

00:47:42.310 --> 00:47:46.945
if you develop that skill you'll do great,
in personal as well as in life.

00:47:46.945 --> 00:47:49.260
More questions.

00:47:49.260 --> 00:47:50.450
There was one over here.

00:47:53.184 --> 00:47:56.545
This gentleman, there's one here,
here, and there's a lady in the back.

00:48:03.746 --> 00:48:05.918
Good afternoon.

00:48:07.763 --> 00:48:08.270
MSX.

00:48:08.270 --> 00:48:13.120
You yourself just mentioned that Android
has had a big impact on the cell phones,

00:48:13.120 --> 00:48:15.330
smart cell phones,
penetrating into the world.

00:48:16.340 --> 00:48:20.230
And the big aspect of that was the Android
being distributed almost for free.

00:48:21.660 --> 00:48:23.370
&gt;&gt; Free is always a good starting price.

00:48:24.540 --> 00:48:28.113
&gt;&gt; Great.
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:48:28.113 --> 00:48:29.355
&gt;&gt; Going to autonomous

00:48:29.355 --> 00:48:34.260
technology on four-wheelers, is that
your intention, going there as well?

00:48:34.260 --> 00:48:38.880
Or how do you see that percolating into
the world, and how do you envision that?

00:48:38.880 --> 00:48:41.230
&gt;&gt; Different industrial structure,
different strategy,

00:48:41.230 --> 00:48:43.240
we haven't really determined
exactly how to do it.

00:48:43.240 --> 00:48:44.539
We're just trying to
get everything to work.

00:48:46.453 --> 00:48:50.030
So in phones,
the story is pretty straightforward.

00:48:51.300 --> 00:48:53.380
There were a number of
initiatives under way.

00:48:55.100 --> 00:48:57.730
Apple's initiative, very strong player,

00:48:57.730 --> 00:49:01.670
clearly not licensable by its competitors,
right?

00:49:01.670 --> 00:49:06.480
When you call Apple, they wouldn't return
the phone, literally the phone, sorry for

00:49:06.480 --> 00:49:10.420
the pun, to license the technology.

00:49:10.420 --> 00:49:17.684
And so we were there with a solution that
had the right price, free, and worked.

00:49:17.684 --> 00:49:20.500
And it didn't have the encumbrances
that the other choices did.

00:49:20.500 --> 00:49:26.798
And that's produced a platform that
is well more than a billion users.

00:49:26.798 --> 00:49:30.290
Most of the studies indicate that
it's a vast majority of all the smart

00:49:30.290 --> 00:49:32.200
phones in the world are using Android.

00:49:32.200 --> 00:49:35.826
And we've now taken Android into other
markets, including television and cars and

00:49:35.826 --> 00:49:36.654
things like that.

00:49:36.654 --> 00:49:38.318
I don't know how far we'll
go into the other markets.

00:49:41.344 --> 00:49:43.440
&gt;&gt; Has to leave us now.

00:49:43.440 --> 00:49:45.038
So if you have any more questions.

00:49:45.038 --> 00:49:47.766
&gt;&gt; I think the lady in
the back had a question.

00:49:47.766 --> 00:49:48.846
&gt;&gt; Even better.

00:49:48.846 --> 00:49:49.934
&gt;&gt; Right there.

00:49:49.934 --> 00:49:55.241
&gt;&gt; Why don't you have the- yes,
right up front.

00:49:55.241 --> 00:49:58.240
Why don't you have the last question.

00:49:59.330 --> 00:49:59.920
&gt;&gt; Thank you.

00:49:59.920 --> 00:50:03.846
My name is Tatiana Nunez, from Mexico.

00:50:03.846 --> 00:50:08.996
We're very excited about
the Calico research that

00:50:08.996 --> 00:50:14.024
you are developing to help
to enlarge the lifespan

00:50:14.024 --> 00:50:18.520
of humans, and-
&gt;&gt; I'm more interested than you are,

00:50:18.520 --> 00:50:19.062
because I'm older.

00:50:19.062 --> 00:50:20.715
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So.

00:50:20.715 --> 00:50:26.172
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; That's very exciting and

00:50:26.172 --> 00:50:31.104
our question is about if there is already

00:50:31.104 --> 00:50:35.457
a business strategy around that and

00:50:35.457 --> 00:50:42.170
how are you planning to
have a revenue about this?

00:50:42.170 --> 00:50:43.220
&gt;&gt; So the answer is not yet.

00:50:44.880 --> 00:50:48.580
For those of you who
haven't been following it,

00:50:48.580 --> 00:50:52.050
Google has two large
initiatives in the health area.

00:50:52.050 --> 00:50:55.670
One is called Verily, which is not Calico.

00:50:55.670 --> 00:50:59.440
And Verily is about, today anyway,
about medical devices.

00:50:59.440 --> 00:51:05.410
We did a fantastic contact lens that
could help you with blood glucose,

00:51:05.410 --> 00:51:08.050
which is a problem that
affects diabetics and others.

00:51:09.100 --> 00:51:13.569
They've since announced many new products
in this sort of- think of it as medical

00:51:13.569 --> 00:51:17.996
device, medical instrumentation and
everybody wants to partner with Verily.

00:51:17.996 --> 00:51:18.680
They're doing very well.

00:51:20.030 --> 00:51:23.051
Calico is more today
of a research project,

00:51:23.051 --> 00:51:26.818
try to understand the underlying
processes of aging.

00:51:26.818 --> 00:51:30.371
I've been briefed on it,
but I could not accurately

00:51:30.371 --> 00:51:34.754
describe the science that they're doing,
even if I wanted to.

00:51:34.754 --> 00:51:39.424
But they have the labs, they're
looking at the various chemical and

00:51:39.424 --> 00:51:44.257
biological processes, can they be
slowed or effected by treatments,

00:51:44.257 --> 00:51:47.130
new drugs, that kind of things?

00:51:47.130 --> 00:51:50.740
If it works, right,
which we'll know some years from now,

00:51:50.740 --> 00:51:56.150
it's a classic biotech,
the economics should be incredible, right?

00:51:56.150 --> 00:51:59.910
Because this will be like a brand new drug
that everyone would want to take, right?

00:51:59.910 --> 00:52:01.830
Who doesn't want to live longer?

00:52:01.830 --> 00:52:04.936
And if it doesn't work, well,
we'll have tried valiantly and

00:52:04.936 --> 00:52:08.861
we'll have advanced the science and the
research in that area in a material way.

00:52:08.861 --> 00:52:12.973
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] is that we
recommend catching Eric at

00:52:12.973 --> 00:52:16.925
Burning Man at the end of
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:52:16.925 --> 00:52:18.124
&gt;&gt; But otherwise thank you so

00:52:18.124 --> 00:52:19.165
much Eric for coming.

00:52:19.165 --> 00:52:20.237
&gt;&gt; Thank you all.

00:52:20.237 --> 00:52:22.973
Please have a great spring, okay, see you.

00:52:22.973 --> 00:52:29.782
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:52:29.782 --> 00:52:30.282
[MUSIC]

